    "lem-cdr-weak-injectivity-and-auxiliary-pattern-reduction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-satellite-induced-concordance-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let V=S¹×D² be the standard oriented solid torus, with oriented core, meridional disk and product longitude. A pattern is a smooth oriented knot P in the interior of V. Its winding number w(P) is its algebraic intersection with an oriented meridional disk, equivalently the coefficient of the core in [P]∈H₁(V;Z). For a smooth oriented knot K in S³, let e_K:V→S³ be a zero-framed tubular embedding carrying the core to K and carrying the meridian and product longitude to the positive meridian and preferred longitude of K. The satellite knot P(K) is e_K(P).\n\nWhen concordance preservation has been established, the formula [K]↦[P(K)] defines a set map on each of C_sm, C_top, C_ex and C^{1/n}. The solid-torus meridian is strong winding number ±1 for P if, after V is embedded as the standard unknotted solid torus and P(U) denotes the resulting closure, that meridian normally generates π₁(S³\\P(U)). Normal generation means that the smallest normal subgroup containing the element is the whole group. Strong winding number ±1 is a separate condition from ordinary winding number ±1. No additivity or homomorphism property is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-cdr-zero-surgery-pattern-homology-cobordism",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let P be an oriented pattern of winding n≠0 and K an oriented knot. If P(U) bounds a smooth proper disk in a compact smooth 4-manifold B with boundary S³ and H_i(S³;R_n)→H_i(B;R_n) is an isomorphism for every i, then for every K the zero surgeries M(P(K)) and M(K) are smoothly R_n-homology cobordant, with preferred meridian carried to preferred meridian. If P has strong winding number ±1 and P(U) is pseudo-slice (respectively locally flat topologically slice), then M(P(K)) and M(K) are smoothly (respectively topologically) homology cobordant through a 4-manifold whose fundamental group is normally generated by the image of the fundamental group of either boundary component. The category and closure hypotheses are separate and cannot be inferred from winding alone.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-cdr-satellite-concordance-categories",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let knots be smooth oriented knots in the standard oriented S³ and let I=[0,1]. Let C_sm be their smooth concordance classes, where equivalence is witnessed by a smooth proper annulus in S³×I. Let C_top be their locally flat topological concordance classes in that product. Let C_ex be their exotic-smooth concordance classes: equivalence is witnessed by a smooth proper annulus in a compact smooth oriented 4-manifold W together with an orientation-preserving homeomorphism of pairs (W,∂W)≅(S³×I,S³×{0,1}) matching the endpoint knots. For a nonzero integer n set R_n=Z[1/|n|]. Let C^{1/n} be the classes under equivalence witnessed by a smooth proper annulus in a compact smooth oriented 4-manifold W with boundary the two standard endpoint 3-spheres such that each boundary inclusion induces an isomorphism H_i(S³;R_n)→H_i(W;R_n) for every i. A knot is pseudo-slice if it bounds a smooth proper disk in a compact smooth 4-manifold homeomorphic to B⁴, with the boundary identified with the standard S³. A knot is Z[1/|n|]-slice if it bounds a smooth proper disk in a compact smooth 4-manifold whose boundary inclusion is an isomorphism on homology with coefficients R_n. These are sets of equivalence classes; this definition alone asserts no group structure.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cdr-concordance-categories-form-abelian-groups",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC_ω. Under connected sum, each of C_sm, C_top, C_ex and C^{1/n} is an abelian group. The identity is the class of the unknot, and the inverse of [K] is the class of the reverse mirror of K. Each category retains the ambient and coefficient conditions in its definition.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
