          "claim": "For the cap assertion, use the support class of [F3] on $K\\cup L$, whose restrictions are the two smaller support classes. Subdivide a finite representative orientation chain so that every simplex lies either in $U$ or outside $K$, and either in $V$ or outside $L$; the two open covers and the finite chain allow one common finite subdivision by [F2]. If $\\alpha=\\alpha_U+\\alpha_V$ is a degree-$p$ supported cocycle lifted by the short exact sequence of step 1.1, then $\\alpha_U\\cap z$ is a chain in $U$ and $\\alpha_V\\cap z$ is a chain in $V$: the other simplices lie outside the corresponding support and their front evaluations vanish. Their sum is $\\alpha\\cap z$. The supported cohomology connector is represented by $\\delta\\alpha_U=-\\delta\\alpha_V$ in the intersection complex. The homology connector is represented by $\\partial(\\alpha_U\\cap z)$. Since $\\partial z$ lies outside $K\\cup L$, [F4] gives $\\partial(\\alpha_U\\cap z)=(-1)^{p+1}(\\delta\\alpha_U)\\cap z$. To check the kernel-to-support comparison as well, put $\\gamma=\\delta\\alpha_U$ and use $\\rho=1-\\partial D-D\\partial$ from step 1.1. The actual cochain supported in $K\\cap L$ is $\\gamma\\rho$. Since $\\gamma$ is a cocycle, $\\gamma\\rho-\\gamma=\\delta\\beta$ with $\\beta=-\\gamma D$. The homotopy $D$ vanishes on every simplex contained either outside $K$ or outside $L$, so $\\beta$ vanishes there too. After the same cover subdivision, $\\beta\\cap z$ is a chain in $U\\cap V$ and $\\beta\\cap\\partial z=0$. The identity [F4] shows that $(\\gamma\\rho)\\cap z-\\gamma\\cap z$ is the boundary $(-1)^{p+1}\\partial(\\beta\\cap z)$ in that intersection. Thus the computed connector represents the cap of the actual supported cohomology class, not just of its small-complex model. This is the claimed connecting-square sign. The other squares commute by addition, the same support restrictions, and the cap formula.",
