      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For a singular simplex $\\sigma:[v_0,\\ldots,v_n]\\to M$, put its coefficient in $\\mathcal R_{\\sigma(v_0)}$ and take the direct sum of those right modules over all singular simplices. For $n\\ge1$ define $d(c\\sigma)=T_{01}(c)\\,\\sigma[1,\\ldots,n]+\\sum_{i=1}^n(-1)^i c\\,\\sigma[0,\\ldots,\\widehat i,\\ldots,n]$, and let the degree-zero differential be zero. Every face map is right-linear because transport is, so this defines right-$R$ linear differentials without assuming that $R$ is commutative. To check $d^2=0$, pair the two terms deleting vertices $i<j$. Their signs are $(-1)^{i+j-1}$ and $(-1)^{i+j}$. If $i>0$, both coefficients are $c$. If $i=0<1<j$, both coefficients are $T_{01}(c)$. If $(i,j)=(0,1)$, the coefficients are $T_{12}T_{01}(c)$ and $T_{02}(c)$, which agree because the two-edge path is endpoint-fixed homotopic to the direct edge in the simplex and transport is a groupoid functor. Thus every pair cancels. This includes degenerate simplices and degree one, where the next differential is zero. These are the underlying abelian local chains of [F1], now equipped with their verified right-$R$ structure.",
