          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "First construct a compatible metric from finite data. Compactness supplies finitely many coordinate balls $V_i$ whose closures lie in larger coordinate balls with charts $\\varphi_i$; take their coordinate images concentric balls of radii $r_i<R_i$. A continuous radial function $\\chi_i$ equals one on $\\overline V_i$, decreases linearly to zero before the larger chart boundary, and is zero elsewhere on $M$. The finitely many functions $\\chi_i$ and $\\chi_i\\varphi_i$, extended by zero, are continuous because their coordinate supports are compactly contained in the charts. Their combined map $F$ into a finite Euclidean space is injective: for $x$, choose $i$ with $x\\in V_i$, so $\\chi_i(x)=1$; equality $F(x)=F(y)$ forces $\\chi_i(y)=1$ and $\\varphi_i(x)=\\varphi_i(y)$, hence $x=y$. A continuous injection of a compact space into a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism onto its image: images of closed subsets are compact and hence closed. Therefore $d(x,y)=|F(x)-F(y)|$ is a compatible metric. All choices so far are finite.",
