          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix the paths from [F1]. For a degree-one cocycle $\\alpha$ in a local system $\\mathcal E$, the two-simplex identity of [F2] gives $\\alpha(uv)=\\alpha(u)+T_u^{-1}\\alpha(v)$ for concatenated paths, with the second term transported to the initial fiber. A triangle realizing the concatenation proves this formula, and a triangulated homotopy square proves invariance for endpoint-fixed path homotopies; common internal edges cancel with their inverse transports. A constant two-simplex shows that a cocycle evaluates to zero on a constant path, and the concatenation formula then handles reversed paths. Define a zero-cochain $v_x=T_{\\gamma_x}\\alpha(\\gamma_x)$ and $v_o=0$. The normalized cocycle $\\alpha^0=\\alpha-\\delta v$ vanishes on every $\\gamma_x$. If $g_\\sigma=[\\gamma_x\\sigma\\overline\\gamma_y]$ for a path $\\sigma:x\\to y$, the concatenation formula consequently gives $\\alpha^0(\\sigma)=T_{\\gamma_x}d(g_\\sigma)$, where $d(g)=\\alpha^0(g)$ on based loops. Here $d(gh)=d(g)+\\rho(g)d(h)$ with $\\rho(g)=T_g^{-1}$ in the cohomological convention. This is a crossed homomorphism; conversely that formula assigns a normalized cocycle to every such $d$, since the three edges of a singular two-simplex satisfy the same loop relation.",
