      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the finite-atlas paths of [F1] to transport the supplied base vector $1$ to a frame $e_x$ in every fiber. Two paths to $x$ give frames $e_x$ and $e'_x=e_xv$ where $v$ is unitary, because their difference is a based loop. Define $h_x(e_xa,e_xb)=\\bar a b$. In the second frame the coordinates are $v^{-1}a,v^{-1}b$, and\n$$\\overline{v^{-1}a}\\,v^{-1}b=\\bar a(\\bar v)^{-1}v^{-1}b=\\bar a b.$$\nThus this definition is path-independent and every transport preserves $h$. The finite-atlas rule supplies all the paths by a formula, so no infinite family of choices is required. The resulting frames are unitary and their transition units are unitary.",
