          "claim": "Suppose $N\\trianglelefteq L$, and both $N$ and $L/N$ have the finite-free property. Let $T:k[L]^m\\to k[L]^n$ have injective augmentation. Applying the quotient-ring map $k[L]\\to k[L/N]$ gives $T_{L/N}$ with the same augmented matrix, hence injective. If $v\\in\\ker T$, its finitely many coordinates have finite group supports. Choose representatives for the finitely many right-$N$ cosets meeting those supports. In the input, the vectors $e_i g$ for these representatives form a basis of a finite free right-$k[N]$ submodule containing $v$. The finitely many images $T(e_i g)$ are contained in another finite free right-$k[N]$ submodule of the target, obtained by choosing representatives for their finitely many support cosets. Normality ensures that the quotient-ring map sends these coset bases to distinct quotient group elements. Thus the restricted finite $k[N]$ matrix has augmentation equal to the $k$-linear restriction of $T_{L/N}$ to the corresponding finite span of quotient basis elements. That restriction is injective, so the finite-free property of $N$ makes the restricted matrix injective. Consequently $v=0$. Only finite sets of coset representatives were selected; no global transversal or splitting was used.",
